  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nIsolated light showers have developed over the coastal waters this\nmorning as an upper level trough continues to progress inland\nacross the Western US. Latest hi-res guidance suggests most of \nthese showers will move south and remain off the coast, with only \naround a 10% chance of these showers moving into the coastal \nareas. Any accumulation from these showers will be a couple \nhundredths of an inch or less. Otherwise cool today with highs \naround 3 to 7 degrees below normal. \n\nWeak Santa Ana winds will develop tonight behind the departing\ntrough. A second short wave trough is forecast to dig south across\nthe Great Basin into Arizona Friday and Saturday, driving\nrelatively stronger Santa Ana winds. Despite only being a few days\naway, there remains a sizable difference in the forecast pressure\ngradients between the GEFS and EC Ensemble, with the EC\nforecasting a stronger event. The CW3E ensemble currently shows a\n40% chance of a weak-to-moderate event on Saturday (gusts around\n25-40 mph in the wind prone areas), but only a 10% chance of a\nmoderate event (gusts around 35-50 mph). For now winds do not\nappear to be much of a concern, but this offshore flow combined\nwith an upper level ridge building in from the west will bring\nconsiderable warming. By Friday, highs will be anywhere from 5 to\n10 degrees above normal west of the mountains with temps in the\nlow to mid 80s for the coastal areas and valleys. \n\nWarming continues into Sunday under upper level ridging and weak \noffshore flow with highs reaching the upper 80s for the inland\ncoastal areas/western valleys and in general 5-15 degrees above\nnormal for the coasts/valleys, but only a couple degrees above\nnormal for the mountains and deserts. HeatRisk is forecast to\nreach Moderate for the coastal areas Saturday through Monday.\nGradual cooling Tuesday and Wednesday as offshore flow weakens and\neventually turns back onshore.",
